# Day 11 detection: fires on a burst of TCP SYN packets to target's port
# 5000 from the same source within a short window -- the exact half-open
# SYN-scan pattern `nmap -sS` produces, and the same technique Day 2
# (content/day02-networking.md) taught against a different target.
#
# Signature-based, by construction: this rule matches a SPECIFIC packet
# shape (bare SYN, no data, five-in-ten-seconds from one source) rather
# than any learned baseline of "normal" traffic. See content/
# day11-detection.md Section 1 and Drill 4 for why that distinction
# matters -- and for what an anomaly-based scan detector would need
# instead (a baseline of normal connection rates per source).
#
# threshold:type both -- fires once per matching window per source, not
# once per matching packet (avoids one alert per SYN once the threshold
# is already crossed).
alert tcp any any -> any 5000 (msg:"Possible port scan against target:5000 (SYN burst)"; flags:S; threshold:type both, track by_src, count 5, seconds 10; classtype:attempted-recon; sid:1000001; rev:1;)
